:false,"renderedWidth":507,"renderedHeight":405,"naturalWidth":507,"naturalHeight":405,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 360 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":21760},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/s6012a1f2.gif","alt":"The figure shows recreational water-associated outbreaks that were reported in the United States during 2007-2008; 86.6% of these outbreaks were associated with treated water and 13.4% with untreated water. Predominant illnesses included acute gastrointestinal illness (60.4%); illnesses, conditions, or symptoms related to the skin (17.9%), acute respiratory illness (12.7%); illnesses, conditions, or symptoms related to the ears (0.7%), multiple illnesses (7.5%); and other (0.7%). Route of entry was ingestion (60.4%), contact (18.7%), inhalation (13.4%), combined (4.5%), and unknown (3.0%) Etiology was parasitic (50.7%), bacterial (7.5% Legionella and 9.0% non-Legionella), chemnical/toxin (6.7%), viral (3.7%), and multiple (0.7%).","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":621,"renderedHeight":425,"naturalWidth":621,"naturalHeight":425,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 739 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":27051},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/s6012a1f3.gif","alt":"The figure shows the number of waterborne disease outbreaks associated with recreational water that were reported in the United States during 2007-2008, by predominant illness and month. The predominant illness is the category of illness reported by ≥50 of ill respondents. The majority of outbreaks occurred during June, July, and August. Predominant illnesses included acute gastrointestinal illness; illnesses, conditions, or symptoms related to the skin , acute respiratory illness; illnesses, conditions, or symptoms related to the ears, multiple illnesses; and other.","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":611,"renderedHeight":290,"naturalWidth":611,"naturalHeight":290,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 573 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":13468},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/s6012a1f4.gif","alt":"The figure shows recreational water-associated outbreaks of acute gastrointestinal illness (AGI) that were reported in the United States during 2007-2008, by type of exposure and etiology; 70 (86.4%) of the 81 exposures of AGI were associated with treated water and 11 (13.6%) with untreated water. Cryptosporidium spp. accounted for 74.1% of these exposures.","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":622,"renderedHeight":503,"naturalWidth":622,"naturalHeight":503,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 359 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":38064},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/s6012a1f5.gif","alt":"The figure shows the distribution of primary contributing factors reported to the Hazardous Substance Emergency Events Surveillance System for pool chemical-associated health events for 14 states (Colorado, Florida, Iowa, Louisiana, Michigan, Minnesota, North Carolina, New Jersey, New York, Oregon, Texas, Utah, Washington, and Wisconsin) during 2007-2008. The primary contributing factor for all 88 events was equipment failure (35.2%), human error (58.0%), intentional/illegal act (5.7%), and other (1.1%). The primary contributing factor for 52 events in which persons were injured was human error (69.2%) and equipment failure (30.8%).","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":621,"renderedHeight":224,"naturalWidth":621,"naturalHeight":224,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 640 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":14073},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/s6012a1f6.gif","alt":"The figure shows the number of emergency department visits for injuries attributed to pool chemicals reported to the National Electronic Injury Surveillance System during 1999-2008.","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":522,"renderedHeight":370,"naturalWidth":522,"naturalHeight":370,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":12087},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_out.png","alt":"External Web Site Icon","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":10,"renderedHeight":10,"naturalWidth":10,"naturalHeight":10,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":232},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_out.png","alt":"External Web Site Icon","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":10,"renderedHeight":10,"naturalWidth":10,"naturalHeight":10,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":232},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/s6012a1f7.gif","alt":"The figure shows the number of vibriosis cases associated with recreational water exposure that were reported in the United States during 2007-2008, by species and month. A total of 236 cases were reported. The greatest numbers of cases occurred in May, June, July, August, and September.","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":512,"renderedHeight":335,"naturalWidth":512,"naturalHeight":335,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 288 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":12312},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/s6012a1f8.gif","alt":"The figure shows a map of the United States indicating where the 236 reported cases of vibriosis associated with recreational water exposure occurred during 2007-2008. The numbers shown by state are largely dependent on surveillance and reporting activities in individual states and do not necessarily indicate the true incidence of vibriosis cases.","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":503,"renderedHeight":434,"naturalWidth":503,"naturalHeight":434,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 349 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":20952},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/s6012a1f9.gif","alt":"The figure shows the number of waterborne disease outbreaks associated with recreational water that were reported in the United States during 1978-2008, by predominant illness (the category of illness reported by ≥50 of ill respondents) and year. Predominant illnesses included acute gastrointestinal illness; acute respiratory illness; illnesses, conditions, or symptoms related to the skin; and other, which includes bronchitis; illnesses, conditions, or symptoms related to the ears; illnesses, conditions, or symptoms related to the eyes; hepatitis; leptospirosis; meningitis; meningoencephalitis; and multiple predominant illnesses.","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":616,"renderedHeight":246,"naturalWidth":616,"naturalHeight":246,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 637 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":17592},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/s6012a1f10.gif","alt":"The figure shows the number of outbreaks of acute gastrointestinal illness associated with recreational water that were reported in the United States during 1978-2008. Of the 341 outbreaks reported, the highest numbers were reported during 2007-2008.","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":615,"renderedHeight":252,"naturalWidth":615,"naturalHeight":252,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":13993},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/s6012a1f11.gif","alt":"The figure shows recreational water-associated outbreaks of acute gastrointestinal illness that were reported in the United States during 1999-2008, by type of exposure and etiology. Of 228 exposures, treated water accounted for 71.9% and untreated water for 28.1%. Cryptosporidium spp. were associated with 57.0% of the 228 exposures and with 74.4% of the 164 exposures involving treated water.","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":621,"renderedHeight":427,"naturalWidth":621,"naturalHeight":427,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 395 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":31707},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_pdf.gif","alt":"Adobe PDF file","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":16,"renderedHeight":16,"naturalWidth":16,"naturalHeight":16,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":6058},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_word.gif","alt":"Microsoft Word file","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":16,"renderedHeight":16,"naturalWid